  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Objective, Scope & Out-of-Scope Items" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Classification — the Primary-Purpose Test & Unit of Account" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Definitions" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Recognition — the Three-Part Test" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Subsequent Expenditures" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Initial Measurement — Deemed Cost vs Cost" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Subsequent Measurement — Historical Cost & Current Value Models" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Revaluation Mechanics" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Depreciation — the Indefinite-Life Rebuttable Presumption" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Impairment & Derecognition" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Reclassification" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Presentation — Display & Core Disclosures" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Disclosure — Current Value Measurement & Unrecognized Resources" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Worked Example — Determining Current Operational Value" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Worked Example — Comparing Transition Methods" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Control Assessment — Water & Living Resources" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Effective Date & Transition" },
        { id: "s18", label: "Relationship to GFSM 2014 — No IAS/IFRS Equivalent" },
        { id: "s19", label: "Statement / Process Mapping" },
        { id: "s20", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s21", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 51 Tangible Natural Resources Held for Conservation is the current, active standard issued by the IPSASB prescribing the accounting treatment, display, and disclosure for naturally occurring tangible assets held primarily for conservation. Effective for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2028."
    historical="IPSAS 51 is a landmark, first-of-its-kind standard with no direct counterpart in IFRS literature. Developed independently by the IPSASB, it benchmarks against the Government Finance Statistics Manual 2014 (GFSM 2014) and introduces a rebuttable presumption of indefinite useful life, deemed cost via current operational value under IPSAS 46, separate balance sheet presentation under amended IPSAS 1 para. 88(ba), and tailored disclosure carve-outs for rare and endangered resources."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — OBJECTIVE, SCOPE & OUT-OF-SCOPE ITEMS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Scope &amp; Out-of-Scope Items (paras. 1–5)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Objective — the newest Standard in the entire Handbook</div>
      <div class="principle">Establishes principles for reporting relevant information about the nature, amounts, timing, and uncertainties arising from TANGIBLE NATURAL RESOURCES HELD FOR CONSERVATION — issued January 2026, a genuinely novel topic with no direct predecessor anywhere in the accrual IPSAS body of literature.</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Sets out recognition, measurement, and presentation requirements, and requires disclosures enabling users to evaluate the nature/risks of these resources AND their effect on financial position, performance, and cash flows (para. 2).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely residual scope — everything else has its own Standard already</div>
      <div class="cross-list">
        <span>Inventories (IPSAS 12) &middot; Investment property (IPSAS 16)</span>
        <span>Biological assets under active biological transformation for sale (IPSAS 27)</span>
        <span>Service concession assets (IPSAS 32) &middot; Leases (IPSAS 43)</span>
        <span>Assets classified held for sale (IPSAS 44)</span>
        <span>PP&amp;E used in production/supply/rental/administration (IPSAS 45)</span>
        <span>Exploration and evaluation assets (IPSAS 50)</span>
        <span>Obligations relating to these resources — governed by IPSAS 19 instead (para. 5)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — CLASSIFICATION: THE PRIMARY-PURPOSE TEST & UNIT OF ACCOUNT ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Classification — the Primary-Purpose Test &amp; Unit of Account (paras. 6–7, AG2–AG4)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">One resource, potentially multiple units of account</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">A resource may consist of MULTIPLE units of account, each classified INDIVIDUALLY based on the entity's primary intended reason for holding THAT unit. Only units meeting the &sect;3 definition fall within this Standard's scope — others are accounted for under whichever IPSAS matches their own primary purpose (para. 6).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Two required characteristics, and the heritage-asset distinction</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>The asset must be NATURALLY OCCURRING</span>
        <span>It must be held PRIMARILY FOR CONSERVATION</span>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--flush">IPSAS 45 heritage assets (historic buildings, monuments, works of art) can overlap conceptually — preserved for present/future generations due to rarity or significance — but are NOT naturally occurring, so they fail this Standard's first characteristic and stay within IPSAS 45 (&sect;16 in that Standard's own poster now explicitly excludes environmental/natural heritage features as a consequential amendment) (para. AG4).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — DEFINITIONS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Definitions (paras. 8–9, AG5–AG13)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The core definition — deceptively simple</div>
      <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
        <div class="glossary-term">Tangible natural resource held for conservation</div>
        <div class="glossary-def">A naturally occurring tangible asset that is managed to PREVENT its degradation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">"Naturally occurring" = living and non-living components of the Earth constituting the biophysical/geophysical environment (para. AG5). "Conservation" (used interchangeably with "preservation" elsewhere) may mean simply restricting access, or actively managing growth/restoration where a resource needs rehabilitation (paras. AG6–AG7).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">What "degradation" covers — and doesn't</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Depletion from active use/consumption, even by a THIRD PARTY</span>
        <span>Destruction (e.g. forest fires, deliberate or accidental)</span>
        <span>Other decline events, such as pollution (para. AG10)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="callout info callout--flush">NATURAL population deaths from age/disease within a growing population are generally NOT degradation (para. AG11). An INCREASE in service potential from natural growth is a possible conservation outcome — but not a REQUIRED one (para. AG12).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — RECOGNITION: THE THREE-PART TEST ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Recognition — the Three-Part Test (paras. 10–13, AG19–AG24)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The criteria (para. 10)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>PROBABLE that service potential will flow to the entity</span>
        <span>The entity CONTROLS the resource as a result of PAST EVENTS</span>
        <span>The resource can be measured RELIABLY</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Note: "service potential" ONLY — not "economic benefits" — since these resources are held purely for OPERATIONAL capacity (Basis for Conclusions BC23), unlike most other tangible-asset Standards in this library which reference both.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Control indicators — no single one decisive (para. AG22–AG23)</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Legal ownership</span>
        <span>Ability to grant/deny/restrict access</span>
        <span>The means to achieve the entity's objectives with the resource</span>
        <span>An enforceable right to the resource's service potential</span>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Control may exist even where SOME indicators fail — and may be ABSENT even where MOST are satisfied. Legal ownership alone is not decisive; substance over form governs where ownership and service-potential rights diverge.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — SUBSEQUENT EXPENDITURES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Subsequent Expenditures — Maintenance vs. Incremental Service Potential (paras. 14–16)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Day-to-day maintenance — always expensed</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Costs that don't create INCREMENTAL service potential — day-to-day maintenance (rangers protecting a resource, chemical treatment) and research (IPSAS 31 sense) — are expensed as incurred (para. 14).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Expansion — capitalized, regardless of prior recognition status</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Acquiring ADDITIONAL parcels of land to expand a conservation area IS capitalized — it generates incremental service potential likely to flow to the entity (para. 15). This applies EVEN IF the underlying resource was never initially recognized (e.g. because its cost/deemed cost couldn't be measured reliably) — each subsequent expenditure is independently tested against the &sect;4 recognition criteria (para. 16).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — INITIAL MEASUREMENT: DEEMED COST VS COST ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Initial Measurement — Deemed Cost vs. Cost (paras. 17–23)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Two paths, split by transaction type</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">NOT an orderly-market transaction (incl. non-exchange) &rarr; DEEMED COST = current OPERATIONAL value, per IPSAS 46 (para. 17)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">EXCHANGE transaction (rare in practice for these resources) &rarr; COST — purchase price + directly attributable costs (paras. 19–20)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Deemed-cost measurement at initial recognition does NOT constitute a revaluation — the &sect;8 revaluation mechanics apply only to LATER elective revaluations (para. 18).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Directly attributable costs, and the capitalization cutoff</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Employee benefit costs directly from acquisition/development (incl. rehabilitation)</span>
        <span>Site preparation, initial delivery/handling, professional fees (para. 21)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Capitalization STOPS once the item is in the location/condition necessary to be conserved as management intends (para. 22) — the same cutoff logic used throughout IPSAS 45.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — SUBSEQUENT MEASUREMENT: HISTORICAL COST & CURRENT VALUE MODELS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Subsequent Measurement — Historical Cost &amp; Current Value Models (paras. 24–26)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">One policy per class, borrowed directly from IPSAS 46/45's structure</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Choose HISTORICAL COST or CURRENT VALUE — applied to an ENTIRE class, per IPSAS 46</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">If current value ceases to be reliably measurable on a continuing basis &rarr; fall back to historical cost. If it LATER becomes reliably measurable again and the entity elects current value &rarr; treated as a REVALUATION (para. 24).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The two models</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span><strong>Historical cost model</strong> — cost/deemed cost, less accumulated depreciation (if applicable) and accumulated impairment (para. 25)</span>
        <span><strong>Current value model</strong> — revalued amount = current OPERATIONAL value at the revaluation date, less subsequent accumulated depreciation/impairment (para. 26)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — REVALUATION MECHANICS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Revaluation Mechanics (paras. 27–30, AG25–AG29)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Frequency, and the class-level offsetting rule — identical structure to IPSAS 45</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Revalue with SUFFICIENT REGULARITY that carrying amount doesn't materially differ from current operational value — volatile resources may need ANNUAL revaluation; stable/mature ones every three to five years (para. 27, AG25). Individual-asset increases/decreases WITHIN a class are offset against each other; NEVER across different classes (para. 30).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Increases, decreases &amp; recycling</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>INCREASE &rarr; revaluation surplus (net assets/equity) — UNLESS reversing a prior decrease for the SAME class previously in surplus/deficit (para. 28)</span>
        <span>DECREASE &rarr; surplus or deficit — UNLESS offsetting an existing revaluation surplus credit for that SAME class (para. 29)</span>
        <span>Revaluation surplus may transfer to accumulated surplus/deficit on derecognition, or progressively as depreciated — NEVER through surplus or deficit itself (para. AG29)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DEPRECIATION: THE INDEFINITE-LIFE REBUTTABLE PRESUMPTION ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Depreciation — the Indefinite-Life Rebuttable Presumption (paras. 31–40, AG34–AG39)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A REVERSED presumption from IPSAS 45 — genuinely distinctive</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">REBUTTABLE PRESUMPTION: tangible natural resources held for conservation have INDEFINITE useful lives — the opposite default from IPSAS 45's PP&amp;E (finite-life presumption)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">These resources typically aren't used/consumed the way ordinary tangible assets are (para. 31). "Indefinite" still doesn't mean "infinite" — conclusions must rest on objective evidence, not optimistic projections, and should NOT depend on planned future expenditure beyond current-condition maintenance (para. 33) — the same guardrail IPSAS 45 applies to its own indefinite-life PP&amp;E.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Worked examples of finite life, and living-resource population logic</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Land encroached by rising sea levels, facing severe continual flooding risk within a foreseeable period &rarr; FINITE life (para. AG37)</span>
        <span>A conserved geological feature erodes over a geological timescale so long there's no foreseeable service-potential limit &rarr; INDEFINITE (para. AG36)</span>
        <span>Living resources: conserved at POPULATION level, not individual-organism level — a STABLE or GROWING population generally supports indefinite life; a DECLINING endangered population may support finite life instead (para. AG39)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Residual value, useful life, and depreciation method are all reviewed AT LEAST annually — changes are IPSAS 3 accounting estimate changes (paras. 38, 40).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — IMPAIRMENT & DERECOGNITION ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Impairment &amp; Derecognition (paras. 41–46)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Impairment — a straight cross-reference to IPSAS 21</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">No bespoke impairment mechanics — apply IPSAS 21's non-cash-generating-asset framework directly (para. 41). Indefinite-life resources are reviewed ANNUALLY for impairment indicators (para. 42) — the same annual-review discipline seen throughout this library for indefinite-life assets.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Derecognition — the familiar three triggers</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Gain/loss = net disposal proceeds − carrying amount &rarr; surplus or deficit at derecognition</div>
      </div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>On DISPOSAL, when control CEASES, or when NO service potential is expected (para. 43)</span>
        <span>Deferred consideration &rarr; recognized at cash price equivalent; the difference is interest revenue under IPSAS 47 (para. 46)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — RECLASSIFICATION ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Reclassification — Into and Out of Scope (paras. 47–50)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A bidirectional test, following the IPSAS 16 reclassification pattern</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">If a resource (or specific part) is NO LONGER primarily held for conservation &rarr; reclassify based on its NEW primary use. If a resource within ANOTHER Standard's scope becomes primarily held for conservation &rarr; reclassify INTO this Standard (para. 47). Up to the reclassification date, the entity keeps applying the PREVIOUS Standard, considering whether the change itself indicates impairment under IPSAS 21 or 26 (para. 48).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Carrying amount carries over as deemed cost</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Net carrying amount immediately BEFORE reclassification (plus revaluation surplus, if identifiable) becomes the COST for subsequent accounting under the newly-applicable Standard</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">A resource meeting IPSAS 44's held-for-sale criteria is reclassified there instead (para. 50) — routing back into this library's own IPSAS 44 poster.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — PRESENTATION: DISPLAY & CORE DISCLOSURES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Presentation — Display &amp; Core Disclosures (paras. 51–58)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Display — its own SOFP line item</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Displayed as a SEPARATE line item on the statement of financial position, per IPSAS 1 para. 88 — now amended to add subsection (ba) for this exact purpose</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Core disclosures for RECOGNIZED resources (para. 54–57)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>Narrative description — nature/type, location/quantity, significant unit-of-account judgments, significance to entity objectives, and the judgments supporting asset recognition</span>
        <span>Measurement model and basis for gross carrying amount</span>
        <span>If depreciable: WHY, method, residual value, useful lives/rates, gross carrying amount and accumulated depreciation</span>
        <span>A full RECONCILIATION — additions, disposals/held-for-sale, revaluation/impairment increases-decreases, depreciation, reclassifications</span>
        <span>Restrictions on title/use, pledged-as-security amounts, other stewardship responsibilities</span>
        <span>For revalued classes: effective date, independent valuer involvement, revaluation surplus movement, and the SUM of all individual revaluation surpluses/deficits within the class</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — DISCLOSURE: CURRENT VALUE MEASUREMENT & UNRECOGNIZED RESOURCES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Disclosure — Current Value Measurement, Unrecognized Resources &amp; the Rare/Endangered Carve-Out (paras. 59–71)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Current value measurement — the familiar fair value hierarchy package</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Mirrors the fair value disclosure packages seen throughout this library: measurement techniques/inputs, observable vs. unobservable basis, Level 3 reconciliations, valuation process descriptions, and sensitivity narratives (paras. 62–64) — presented in TABULAR format unless another format suits better (para. 67).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Unrecognized resources, and a genuinely distinctive conservation-specific carve-out</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Resources meeting the asset definition but failing reliable measurement &rarr; disclose qualitative info (nature, location, quantity), the measurement DIFFICULTIES, and SIGNIFICANCE to entity objectives (para. 68)</span>
        <span>Stewardship-only resources (unrecognized) &rarr; explain the nature of stewardship rights/responsibilities and the legislation establishing them (para. 71)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>Rare/endangered exemption:</strong> disclosing LOCATION, QUANTITY, or CARRYING AMOUNT of a rare or endangered resource could itself facilitate further endangerment (e.g. poaching coordinates) — an entity MAY withhold that specific detail, disclosing only the general nature of the resource plus the fact AND reason disclosure was limited (paras. 60, 69, AG42–AG43). A genuinely novel disclosure carve-out with no real precedent elsewhere in this library.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — WORKED EXAMPLE: DETERMINING CURRENT OPERATIONAL VALUE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Worked Example — Determining Current Operational Value of a Conservation Forest (Illustrative Example 3)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A government entity obtains a 5,000-hectare conservation woodland via a non-exchange transaction</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush">
        <div class="wk-title">Two acceptable valuation approaches, both permitted under IPSAS 46 — market approach vs. cost approach</div>
        <div class="wk-body">
          <strong>Case A — Market approach (external valuer):</strong> Uses historical purchase data from comparable conservation forests, adjusted for location/size differences, species differences, and average plantation age — cross-checked against physically similar TIMBER-PRODUCTION forests (with purpose differences factored in) as a reasonableness check. <strong class="text-danger">Result: CU28 million.</strong><br /><br />
          <strong>Case B — Cost approach (develop a similar asset):</strong> Estimates the cost to GROW a comparable forest from scratch — a ~20-year growth period, CU4,000/hectare forestation costs (site prep, seedlings, present value of 20 years' labor/maintenance), plus CU1,500/hectare for land with similar characteristics. <strong class="text-danger">Result: 5,000 ha &times; (CU4,000 + CU1,500) = CU27.5 million.</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Both approaches land within ~2% of each other (CU28.0m vs. CU27.5m) — a useful cross-check on estimate reasonableness. Given the estimation uncertainty inherent in either method, the entity must ALSO consider IPSAS 1 paras. 140–148's key-source-of-estimation-uncertainty disclosures, layered on top of this Standard's own &sect;13 current-value disclosure package.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 — WORKED EXAMPLE: COMPARING TRANSITION METHODS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Worked Example — Modified Retrospective vs. Full Retrospective Transition (Illustrative Example 5)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Case A — resource still controlled at transition (historical cost model elected)</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush">
        <div class="wk-title">Resource A: CU50m current value when control was originally obtained (decades ago, unrecognized under the old framework); CU70m current value by 20X2, held continuously through 20X4 initial IPSAS 51 application</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">
          <strong>Modified retrospective</strong> (para. 74(a)): recognize at CU70m (current value AT the date of initial application, Jan 1, 20X4), adjusting opening accumulated surplus/deficit by CU70m.<br /><br />
          <strong>Full retrospective under IPSAS 3</strong> (para. 74(b)): recognize at the ORIGINAL CU50m (value when control was first obtained) — since the entity elected the historical cost model, the SUBSEQUENT CU70m increase is never reflected. Carrying amount and opening accumulated surplus/deficit both stay at CU50m.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Case B — resource sold before transition</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush">
        <div class="wk-title">Same facts, except Resource A was sold in 20X2 for CU70m cash — unrecognized at the time, so the ENTIRE proceeds hit accumulated surplus/deficit as a CU70m gain under the old framework</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">
          <strong>Modified retrospective:</strong> no adjustment at all — the resource wasn't controlled at the Jan 1, 20X4 application date, so it's simply outside scope.<br /><br />
          <strong>Full retrospective:</strong> recognize CU50m at original acquisition (reflected in 20X3 opening accumulated surplus/deficit); the 20X2 sale gain becomes only CU20m (CU70m proceeds − CU50m carrying amount) instead of the original CU70m. <strong class="text-danger">Net result: accumulated surplus/deficit at Jan 1, 20X3 is STILL CU70m either way</strong> (CU50m initial recognition + CU20m gain) — the retrospective method changes WHERE the value shows up (asset vs. gain), not the total.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 — CONTROL ASSESSMENT: WATER & LIVING RESOURCES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Control Assessment — Water &amp; Living Resources (Implementation Guidance IG11–IG17)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Water — free-flowing is (almost) never controllable</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Oceans, seas, lakes, rivers — their physical movement generally CAN'T be controlled by any single entity (IG13). Water in a MANAGED environment (e.g. an underground aquifer) CAN be controlled where the entity has legal ownership conferring enforceable service-potential rights, the MEANS to actively manage volume/flow, and the ability to RESTRICT others' access (IG14).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Living resources — mobility complicates everything</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Plants (immobile) are generally EASIER to demonstrate control over than animals/fish that can freely roam across jurisdictional boundaries (IG15). Control requires the ability to DIRECT the resource's conservation AND restrict others' access — physical barriers, legislation, or security personnel (IG16). An UNFETTERED ability to sell a living resource is a strong control indicator — but ALSO a strong sign the item is really inventory (IPSAS 12) or a biological asset (IPSAS 27) rather than genuinely within THIS Standard's scope (IG17).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 — EFFECTIVE DATE & TRANSITION ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Transition (paras. 72–78)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The most future-dated Standard in this entire library</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Periods beginning on/after January 1, 2028 — earlier application permitted, with disclosure if elected (para. 72)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Deliberately NOT required to be adopted alongside IPSAS 50 or the IPSAS 12 stripping-costs amendments — the Board found no technical interdependency requiring simultaneous adoption (Basis for Conclusions BC44).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Two transition methods — a genuine choice</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span><strong>Modified retrospective</strong> — recognize qualifying resources at deemed cost (current operational value) AS AT the date of initial application; cumulative effect adjusts opening accumulated surplus/deficit. Resources already DERECOGNIZED before that date need NOT be considered (para. 74(a))</span>
        <span><strong>Full retrospective</strong> — apply IPSAS 3's ordinary retrospective restatement instead (para. 74(b))</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">If current value can't be reliably measured at initial application &rarr; the same &sect;13 disclosure-only treatment applies as for ongoing unrecognized resources (para. 76). Reclassified assets carry over at their pre-reclassification carrying amount, incl. any identifiable revaluation surplus (para. 77).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 — RELATIONSHIP TO GFSM 2014: NO IAS/IFRS EQUIVALENT ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Relationship to GFSM 2014 — No IAS/IFRS Equivalent</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely original IPSASB Standard — no IFRS counterpart exists</div>
      <div class="principle">IPSAS 51 has NO IFRS Standard drawn-from relationship — IFRS has never addressed conservation-held natural resources as a distinct asset category. Instead, the Handbook benchmarks it against the Government Finance Statistics Manual 2014 (GFSM 2014), joining IPSAS 22, 42, 48, and 50 as Standards compared to statistical frameworks rather than IFRS.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Comparison</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Recognition/measurement basics</span><span>ALIGNED — both require recognition/measurement, though GFSM 2014 doesn't report these resources SEPARATELY, folding them into the appropriate broader asset category</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Scope breadth</span><span>IPSAS 51 covers ONLY conservation-held resources; GFSM 2014 covers natural resources more BROADLY</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Presentation of value changes</span><span>IPSAS 51: net assets/equity OR surplus/deficit, as appropriate. GFSM 2014: "other economic flows" — a distinct statistical category</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>SOFP display</span><span>IPSAS 51 REQUIRES a separate line item (&sect;12); GFSM 2014 folds these resources into the appropriate nonfinancial-asset category on the balance sheet</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Measurement convergence</span><span>GFSM 2014 uses MARKET VALUE throughout; IPSAS 51's current operational value may closely APPROXIMATE this, but preparers should check for adjustments (ownership transfer costs, exit-price assumptions)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 — STATEMENT / PROCESS MAPPING ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Master decision map</div>
      <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Determine the UNIT OF ACCOUNT — is the resource naturally occurring AND held PRIMARILY for conservation? If not &rarr; route to whichever IPSAS matches its actual primary purpose (&sect;1, &sect;2)</li>
        <li>Apply the THREE recognition criteria — probable service potential, control from past events, reliable measurement. Fails reliable measurement? &rarr; disclose only, don't recognize (&sect;4, &sect;13)</li>
        <li>Measure initial cost/deemed cost — current operational value for non-exchange events, cost for exchange transactions (&sect;6)</li>
        <li>Choose ONE subsequent measurement policy per CLASS — historical cost, or current value (&sect;7, &sect;8)</li>
        <li>Assess useful life — REBUTTABLE PRESUMPTION of INDEFINITE life; depreciate only if genuinely finite (&sect;9)</li>
        <li>Test for impairment under IPSAS 21; on disposal/loss of control/no remaining service potential &rarr; derecognize (&sect;10)</li>
        <li>Primary purpose CHANGES? &rarr; reclassify, carrying amount becomes deemed cost under the new Standard (&sect;11)</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-spaced">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 51 concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Tangible natural resource carrying amount</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Separate line item, per amended IPSAS 1 para. 88(ba)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Depreciation charge (if finite life)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Rare — most resources are indefinite-life</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Revaluation increase</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>Unless reversing a prior class-level decrease in surplus/deficit</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Revaluation decrease</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Unless offset against existing class-level revaluation surplus</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Gain/loss on derecognition</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Net proceeds less carrying amount</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Unrecognized resources meeting the asset definition</span><span><span class="badge be">Notes</span></span><span>Disclosure only — no SOFP recognition</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 20 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 51 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 1</span><span>Amended to add the separate SOFP line item (para. 88(ba)) and expense disclosure item (para. 107(ca)); estimation-uncertainty disclosures for current-value measurements (&sect;12, &sect;14)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 3</span><span>Amended (para. 22) to route initial current-value-policy adoption through the revaluation mechanics, not ordinary policy-change treatment; governs full retrospective transition and estimate-change accounting throughout (&sect;7, &sect;9, &sect;17)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 5</span><span>Allowed alternative for capitalizing deferred-payment interest (&sect;6, AG30)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 12 / 16 / 27</span><span>Each amended to explicitly EXCLUDE tangible natural resources held for conservation from their own scope (&sect;1)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 19</span><span>Governs obligations RELATING to these resources — explicitly excluded from IPSAS 51's own scope (&sect;1)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 21</span><span>THE governing impairment Standard — no bespoke IPSAS 51 impairment mechanics exist (&sect;10)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 31</span><span>Defines "research" for the day-to-day maintenance expense test (&sect;5); governs resources reclassified as intangible</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>Amended with new transitional exemptions (paras. 62D–62E, AG54(m), AG56(j), AG61) for first-time adopters (&sect;17)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 44</span><span>Governs held-for-sale reclassification (&sect;11)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 45</span><span>Structural template for nearly every mechanic in this Standard (measurement models, revaluation, depreciation) — amended (para. 3(e)) to exclude conservation resources, and (AG2) to strip "environmental/natural" from its own heritage asset description (&sect;2, &sect;7–&sect;9)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 46</span><span>Source of the current operational value measurement basis applied throughout (&sect;6, &sect;14)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 47</span><span>Governs deferred-consideration interest revenue on disposal (&sect;10)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 21 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s21"><span class="num">21</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Tangible natural resource held for conservation</div><div class="glossary-def">A naturally occurring tangible asset that is managed to prevent its degradation.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Class of tangible natural resources held for conservation</div><div class="glossary-def">A grouping of resources of similar nature, shown as a single disclosure item.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Degradation</div><div class="glossary-def">A reduction in the overall service potential embodied in a tangible natural resource held for conservation.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Current operational value</div><div class="glossary-def">The amount the entity would pay for the remaining service potential of an asset held primarily for operational capacity.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Naturally occurring</div><div class="glossary-def">The living and non-living components of the Earth constituting the biophysical and geophysical environment.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Endangered / rare resources</div><div class="glossary-def">Resources seriously at risk of extinction, or uncommon/scarce/infrequently encountered, respectively.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position
      <span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span> Statement of financial performance
      <span class="badge be">NA/E</span> Net assets/equity (statement of changes)
    </div>
  </div>
